/*
 * mt6768_battery_table.dtsi -- REPLACEMENT for the generic placeholder file
 * currently included by your battery node:
 *
 *   battery: battery {
 *       compatible = "vivo,battery", "mediatek,bat_gm30";
 *       ...
 *       #include "mediatek/bat_setting/mt6768_battery_table.dtsi"
 *   };
 *
 * Your original file at that path is a GENERIC reference table (whatever
 * mt6768 device it was copied from) -- it parses fine (which is why your
 * log shows "fg_custom_parse_table: battery0_profile_t0, 100, column:3"
 * succeeding), but the actual OCV/voltage/resistance numbers do NOT belong
 * to your real PD1913 battery. Drop this file in at the exact same path,
 * overwriting the generic one, and the #include line in mt6768.dts does
 * not need to change at all.
 *
 * Data source: vivo_battery0_profile_t0..t4 from your own
 * PD1913_battery_table.dtsi (battery id 0, "B-H0 SX 4500").
 * MULTI_BATTERY is unset in your DTS (reads as garbage, see chat) so only
 * battery-id 0's table matters right now regardless -- this covers that.
 *
 * Conversion (verified against the actual parsing code in
 * mtk_battery_core.c: fg_custom_parse_table() copies mah/voltage/resistance
 * straight from the DT ints with NO internal rescaling -- so the DT values
 * themselves must already be pre-scaled the way the ORIGINAL generic
 * mt6768_battery_table.dtsi you were given does it. That file's own data
 * was used as the scaling reference: Z axis 0..29760 over 100 rows
 * regardless of actual mAh (confirmed: same 0..29760 range appears in that
 * file despite it almost certainly not being a 4500 mAh cell -- this is a
 * fixed axis, not capacity-scaled), voltage = mV*10, resistance = mOhm*10):
 *
 *   Z (row i, i=0..99)   = round(i * 29760 / 99)
 *   voltage               = vivo_mV * 10
 *   resistance            = vivo_mOhm * 10
 *
 * vivo's 101 points (idx 0..100) were trimmed to 100 (idx 0..99, dropping
 * the very last fully-depleted point) to match battery0_profile_tX_num=100,
 * which is what fg_table_cust_data.fg_profile[0].size expects (see
 * fg_custom_parse_table -> saddles = fg_table_cust_data.fg_profile[0].size).
 * If that size is NOT 100 in your actual build (check
 * fg_table_cust_data.fg_profile[] initialization / wherever .size gets set
 * before this DTS is parsed), the row count here needs to match it exactly
 * or the lookup will run off the end of the array.
 *
 * Temperature bin mapping (vivo has 5 bins, GM30 here wants 6 --
 * TEMPERATURE_T0..T5 = 50,25,10,0,-6,-10 per your mt6768.dts):
 *   T0 (50C)  <- vivo t4 (50C)   exact match
 *   T1 (25C)  <- vivo t3 (25C)   exact match
 *   T2 (10C)  <- vivo t2 (10C)   exact match
 *   T3 (0C)   <- vivo t1 (0C)    exact match
 *   T4 (-6C)  <- vivo t0 (-10C)  no exact bin, reused (closest available)
 *   T5 (-10C) <- vivo t0 (-10C)  exact match
 * T4 is the only approximation, and only affects behavior in the
 * -10C..0C range -- does not touch normal indoor operation.
 *
 * A companion script (convert_vivo_battery_table.py) is provided alongside
 * this file so you can regenerate it yourself -- e.g. to switch to
 * battery-id 1/2/3 ("B-H0 ATL 4500" / "B-H0 LW 4500") if you find via
 * g_fg_battery_id which one your unit actually reports, or to fix the
 * mapping/scaling if either assumption above turns out wrong on real
 * hardware.
 */

